\label{sec:crosslogic}

The following results extend the primary findings of \S\ref{sec:diagnostic}--\ref{sec:goodhart} to alternative logic types and dataset families; these cross-domain experiments are exploratory in nature.

\begin{figure}[t]

\paragraph{ISR as $(n{-}1)$-coalition Shapley value.}
\label{app:shapley}
Define a cooperative game $(N, v)$ with player set $N = \{1,\ldots,n\}$ (components) and binary utility $v(S) = \mathbf{1}[S(S){=}\textit{proved}]$.
The Shapley value $\phi_i$ averages marginal contributions over all coalition sizes; ISR restricts to the maximal coalition $|S|{=}n{-}1$, giving $\phi_i^{\text{LOO}} = \frac{1}{n}[v(N) - v(N \setminus \{i\})]$.
Summing: $\text{ISR}(x) = \sum_i \phi_i^{\text{LOO}} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_i \mathbf{1}[v(N \setminus \{i\}){=}0]$, recovering Equation~\ref{eq:isr}.
This restriction trades full interaction detection for $O(n)$ cost; the binary solver output makes higher-order interactions unobservable from LOO alone (\S\ref{sec:sta}).

